# Inside a Computer System

## Introduction
- To secure a system, we must first understand it.
- A computer system is like a castle → need to know structure and access points.
- Focus on basic components and their interaction.
- No deep technical knowledge required (fundamentals only).

## Core Components of a Computer

### Motherboard
- Backbone of the system
- Connects all components

### CPU (Central Processing Unit)
- Brain of the computer
- Executes instructions

### RAM (Random Access Memory)
- Short-term memory
- Stores data temporarily while running

### Storage (HDD / SSD)
- Long-term memory
- Stores OS, files, and data

### GPU (Graphics Processing Unit)
- Handles visual output
- Processes graphics

### PSU (Power Supply Unit)
- Supplies power to all components

## Boot Process

### Step 1: Power On
- Power button pressed
- Signal sent to PSU
- System receives power

### Step 2: Firmware Starts (UEFI / BIOS)
- Initializes hardware components
- UEFI replaces older BIOS

### Step 3: POST (Power-On Self Test)
- Checks hardware functionality
- Ensures all components are working

### Step 4: Select Boot Device
- Chooses device (HDD / SSD) to load OS
- Based on priority list

### Step 5: Bootloader Starts
- Loads operating system into RAM
- OS takes control of system

## Key Points
- All components work together to run a system
- Boot process is essential for startup
- Understanding components helps in cybersecurity
- Boot process can be targeted by attackers

# Types of Computers

## Introduction
- Computers are not limited to laptops and phones
- Found in everyday devices (fridge, door, etc.)
- Can be used directly or indirectly
- Includes IoT devices and embedded systems

## Types

### Laptop
- Portable
- Used for everyday tasks

### Desktop
- Fixed location
- Better cooling and performance

### Workstation
- High performance
- Used for professional/precision tasks

### Server
- No dedicated screen/keyboard
- Provides services over a network

## Device-Based Computers

### Smartphone
- Pocket-sized computer
- Optimized for battery and connectivity

### Tablet
- Touch-based device
- Larger screen than smartphone

### IoT Devices
- Connected to network
- Single-purpose devices
- Example: smart doorbell, thermostat

## Embedded Systems
- Built inside other devices
- Perform specific tasks
- Examples: coffee machine, automatic doors

## IoT vs Embedded

### IoT
- Connected to network
- Sends/receives data

### Embedded
- May not be connected
- Works silently inside device
